    "_context": "ng-term outcome for individuals with aplastic anemia. Pernicious anemia, a form of macrocytic hyperchromic anemia, is caused by a lack of intrinsic factor, normally produced by the gastric mucosa. Intrinsic factor is essential to the absorption of vitamin B12 and to the formation of erythrocytes. The condition can vary in its clinical severity. Like many anemias, the complexion may appear pale. Gingival pallor is also common. The tongue is affected in more than 75% of cases; atrophy of the papillae leaves the dorsal surface red, shiny, and smooth.107 It is often painful to eat. Pernicious anemia is treated with vitamin B12 supplementation either orally or by injection. Iron deficiency anemia"
